Filed from kubernetes-ocp (k387, 2026-10-31). Found with Kubernetes::REST 1.110.

When discovery fails after /version answered (an aggregated API group that is down, or the API going away between calls), Kubernetes::REST carps a warning with a Perl source location (REST.pm around lines 953 and 1307). A CLI that already reports the API error as one clean line on STDERR then also shows that carp line.

The caller cannot switch discovery off: Kubernetes::REST::Kubeconfig->api (Kubeconfig.pm:399) takes only a context name, so no constructor options reach Kubernetes::REST. OCP registers its own resource types and does not need discovery for ocp node ls / provider ls.

Possible fixes, either one is enough:
- Kubeconfig->api(context, %options) passes options through to the constructor, so a caller can turn discovery off or tune it.
- Report a discovery failure through a hook or attribute (or a warning without a location) instead of a bare carp.

Done when a caller can get a client from a kubeconfig whose failed discovery adds nothing to STDERR.

Hub decision (perl-developer-bb, 2026-11-06), not a maintainer decision: Kubernetes::REST::Kubeconfig->api($context, %options) passes the options on to the constructor, so a caller can switch discovery off. The POD says so, and a test shows that a switched-off discovery writes nothing to STDERR.
Coordinator's choices (mine): the options reach Kubernetes::REST->new on both paths, kubeconfig and in-cluster fallback. server and credentials are what api() builds from the kubeconfig: passing either as an option croaks, naming the key. An odd-length option list croaks. api(undef, %options) uses the current context. 'Discovery off' is resource_map_from_cluster => 0.

